 "scenario": {
  "description": "Boulder is built at the mouth of Boulder Canyon, where the creek leaves the Rockies and crosses the city past downtown and the university. Between September 9 and 16, 2013 a stalled monsoon plume dropped 17.15 inches of rain on Boulder County — 9.08 inches on September 12 alone, the wettest calendar day in the city's record — and the canyons flashed. Boulder Creek left town at 8,400 ft³/s at the 75th Street gauge, four times its previous record of 2,050 ft³/s, and the flood closed every canyon road out of the city. Here that flood comes out of the canyon at the west edge while the record day's rain falls on the foothills. Boulder Creek is banked only about 3 m deep through town, so it leaves its channel within minutes and runs down the streets beside it.",
